# INF-H8. The base image is named by its digest, so a build takes the same
# image whenever it runs. Docker takes the image the digest names and does
# not use the tag: a pull with a tag that does not exist beside a real digest
# succeeded (2026-10-12). The tag is kept for the reader and for Dependabot,
# which starts from it. The digest is the one the tag had on 2026-10-12, and
# it names the list of the image's builds for each platform, so the line is
# the same on any of them.
#
# The pin is moved by a change to this line. .github/dependabot.yml asks
# Dependabot to propose one. tests/test_image_pins.py reads the line.
#
# The `slim` build of the image: Python 3.10.22 and pip 23.0.1 on Debian 13,
# the releases the full image this file named before has, with 87 Debian
# packages where that has 469. It has no compiler, no make, no git and no
# curl or wget, and of Python's own modules tkinter and turtle do not import
# in it. Nothing in this file, backend/ or alembic/ uses any of those: on
# 2026-10-12 every package in requirements.txt, 38 of them, installed from a
# wheel, and `pip check` found nothing missing. A package added to the lock
# that comes only as source, and cannot be installed without compiling C,
# fails the pip line below (seen with the source of greenlet 3.0.3); the
# full image had gcc to compile it with. One whose C part is optional
# installs without that part (markupsafe 2.0.1). groupadd and useradd, which
# the account further down is made with, are in it. The `compose-stack` job
# in .github/workflows/ci.yml looks for gcc in the image built from this
# file, and fails if it finds it.
#
# The image built from this file is 77 MB as `docker image inspect` gives
# its size and 325 MB as `docker images` lists it; on the full base it was
# 439 MB and 1.73 GB (linux/amd64, Docker 29.7 on the containerd image
# store).
FROM python:3.10-slim-trixie@sha256:6ff506466f8b1e981719b468cc0023b8ef000b983cdec0eafe119d01f379e31d

WORKDIR /app

# INF-H5. requirements.txt is a lock: every package the server runs on, at
# one version, with the hashes of its files. pip stops on a download that
# matches none of its hashes, on a package the lock does not name, and on a
# line without a hash. It does that for any file with a hash in it.
# --require-hashes is for a file with none: pip would install that one as
# given, and with the flag it refuses. (Seen on pip 23.0.1, which the base
# image carried on 2026-10-11.) The lock is compiled from requirements.in,
# which says how.
#
# Not held here: pip itself. It is the release the base image above carries,
# and changes when that line does.
#
# tests/test_dependency_pins.py reads the install line.
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --require-hashes -r requirements.txt

# INF-M6. What the server loads, by name: the migrations and their
# configuration, and the application package. These are the three paths
# docker-compose.yml mounts over, and tests/test_backend_mounts.py fails if
# the two lists part.
#
# Not `COPY . .`. That took the whole repository, less what .dockerignore
# listed, so the image held the frontend's source, the tests, and anything
# that had turned up beside them since the list was written. A new module
# beside backend/ now needs a line here before the image has it.
#
# .dockerignore still decides what comes along inside these directories:
# bytecode, tool caches, a .env, a database file.
COPY alembic.ini ./
COPY alembic ./alembic
COPY backend ./backend

# INF-H4. The server does not run as root. The account is made here with a
# fixed number, and USER names it by that number: a runtime that is told to
# refuse root can check a number without reading the image's /etc/passwd.
#
# Everything above this line ran as root, so the installed packages and the
# files under /app belong to root. The server can read them and cannot change
# them or add to them. It needs to write nothing here: its data is in the
# database.
#
# tests/test_container_users.py reads these two lines, and the `compose-stack`
# job in .github/workflows/ci.yml asks the running container who it is.
RUN groupadd --system --gid 10001 app \
 && useradd --system --uid 10001 --gid app --no-create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin app
USER 10001:10001

# INF-M6. Two settings for the Python that runs the server, and for any other
# Python started in a container of this image.
#
# PYTHONUNBUFFERED: what the server prints goes to the container's log when it
# is printed. Without it Python collects what is written to standard output
# into blocks when that is not a terminal, so the "Database not ready" lines
# of backend/main.py would reach `docker logs` late, or not at all from a
# process that is killed first.
#
# PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE: Python does not try to write a .pyc file beside a
# module it imports. The account above cannot write under /app, so this stops
# the attempts; no file was being written there before either.
#
# They are set down here, after the install, so the pip line above runs as it
# did before them.
ENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1

# INF-H7. How Docker learns whether the server in a container is serving. It
# runs this command in the container: a GET of /health, which backend/main.py
# answers 200 when one query to the database returns and 503 when it raises.
# urlopen raises on a 503, on a refused connection and after its timeout, so
# the command ends with a failure in each case. It is written in Python
# because the image is certain to have it, whatever the base image leaves out.
# A GET, not a HEAD: the route answers a HEAD with 405.
#
# --start-period is longer than the 60 s backend/main.py sleeps between its
# tries to reach a database (30 tries, 2 s apart). Until the first check that
# succeeds, a failure inside the period is not counted, and the check runs
# every 2 s (--start-interval), so a server that is ready early is reported
# soon. From the first success, or from the end of the period: every 10 s,
# and three failures in a row make the container `unhealthy`.
#
# The period is not longer than every wait. A try takes time as well, about
# 4 s each where the database's name does not resolve (measured 2026-10-12),
# and the migrations follow. A server still waiting after the period fails
# its checks like any other, and is reported unhealthy after three.
#
# Unhealthy is a report. Neither `docker run` nor Compose restarts the
# container for it.
#
# docker-compose.yml gives its backend service no check of its own, so this
# is the one it has. tests/test_stack_readiness.py reads this line and runs
# its command against a stand-in server.
HEALTHCHECK --interval=10s --timeout=5s --start-period=90s --start-interval=2s --retries=3 \
  CMD ["python", "-c", "import urllib.request; urllib.request.urlopen('http://127.0.0.1:8000/health', timeout=4)"]

# INF-H1. This is the production command: what the image runs when nothing
# overrides it. It has no file watcher. The development stack asks for one
# itself, with its own `command:` in docker-compose.yml.
#
# --workers 1 is a decision, not a leftover. backend/main.py migrates the
# database in its lifespan handler, which runs once per worker process, so
# more than one worker would run the migrations concurrently. The flag is
# written out because without it uvicorn takes the count from the environment
# (WEB_CONCURRENCY). Raising it needs the migration race closed first
# (DATA-M24).
#
# --proxy-headers: a trusted proxy supplies the client address and scheme
# (X-Forwarded-For, X-Forwarded-Proto). This is uvicorn's default as well, so
# the flag changes no behaviour; it is here so the setting is written down.
# Which peers are trusted comes from the FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS environment
# variable, read by uvicorn when it starts; unset, only loopback is trusted.
# A proxy in another container connects from a different address, so its
# headers are ignored until the variable names it. Do not add
# --forwarded-allow-ips here: a trust-everything value would let any client
# choose the address the server believes it has.
#
# Do not set UVICORN_* variables in this file either. uvicorn takes any option
# from a UVICORN_<OPTION> variable, so one would change this command without
# appearing in it.
#
# tests/test_container_entrypoint.py reads this line and starts a server with
# its flags.
CMD ["uvicorn", "backend.main:app", "--host", "0.0.0.0", "--port", "8000", "--workers", "1", "--proxy-headers"]
